/*!
    \example turtlesim_controller
    \title TurtleSim Controller
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {50}
    \brief Driving the classic ROS 2 \c turtlesim with all four communication
    patterns — publisher, subscriber, service, and action — from one QML app.

    \c{TurtleSim Controller} controls one or more turtles in the classic ROS 2
    \c turtlesim simulator. In a single application it exercises every
    communication pattern the bridge supports: a \e publisher for velocity
    commands, a \e subscriber for pose updates, several \e service \e clients for
    spawning turtles and setting the pen, and an \e action \e client for rotating
    to an absolute heading. It is the best example to read once you have seen the
    \l{Simple Publisher} and \l{Simple Subscriber} basics.

    \image turtlesim_controller/turtlesim_controller.png "The TurtleSim Controller (top) driving turtlesim (bottom), which has drawn the Qt logo using the Draw Qt button."

    \section1 Running the example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment and launch Qt Creator from that shell.
        \li Open \c examples/turtlesim_controller/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator,
            then build and run.
        \li Start the TurtleSim backend in a separate, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node
            \endcode
    \endlist

    \section1 Wrapping a third-party package

    \c turtlesim is not one of the built-in message modules, so the example wraps
    it on demand. In \c CMakeLists.txt, \c qt_ros2_configure_target enables the
    four capabilities the app needs and asks the bridge to generate a QML module
    for the \c turtlesim package:

    \badcode
    qt_ros2_configure_target(appturtlesim_controller
        CAPABILITIES PUBLISHER SUBSCRIBER SERVICE ACTION
        MODULES QtRos2GeometryMessages
        IMPORT_PACKAGES turtlesim
    )
    \endcode

    The generated types become available in QML under
    \c QtRos2.Imported.Turtlesim — imported packages are surfaced beneath the
    \c QtRos2.Imported namespace:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.Imported.Turtlesim
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs as Geom
    \endqml

    \section1 One node per turtle

    All of a turtle's ROS entities are collected in \c TurtleControllerNode.qml,
    a reusable component parameterized by a \c baseName. Because \c turtlesim
    namespaces every topic and service under the turtle's name, the component
    builds its topic strings from \c baseName and shares a single \c Node:

    \qml
    // Velocity command publisher
    Geom.TwistPublisher {
        node: root.rosNode
        topic: `/${root.baseName}/cmd_vel`
    }

    // Pose subscriber
    PoseSubscriber {
        id: poseSubscriber
        node: root.rosNode
        topic: `/${root.baseName}/pose`
    }
    \endqml

    \c{Main.qml} spawns one \c TurtleControllerNode per turtle at runtime and
    switches the panel between them with a combo box, so the same declarative
    block scales from one turtle to many.

    \section1 Services: spawn, kill, and pen control

    Service clients call a service and return a JavaScript \e promise that
    resolves with the response. Spawning a turtle is a single call whose result
    (the assigned name) is used to register a new controller:

    \qml
    SpawnServiceClient {
        id: spawnService
        topic: "/spawn"
    }

    spawnService.callService({ name: "turtle2", x: 5, y: 5 })
        .then(name => root.addTurtle(name))
        .catch(err => console.log("Failed to spawn", err))
    \endqml

    The \e Spawn button is disabled while \c{spawnService.isCallPending} is true,
    and the top-level \c SpawnServiceClient auto-spawns \c turtle1 as soon as its
    \c isServiceReady turns true — a small illustration of driving UI and startup
    logic from service-client state.

    \section2 Chaining promises: the "Draw Qt" button

    Because each service call returns a promise, sequential motions compose
    naturally with \c{.then()}. The \e{Draw Qt} button traces the Qt logo by
    chaining \c SetPen and \c TeleportAbsolute calls into one long promise chain,
    so each stroke waits for the previous teleport to complete before starting:

    \qml
    let promise = moveTo(firstPoint)      // pen off, teleport
    for (let i = 1; i < points.length; i++)
        promise = promise.then(() => lineTo(points[i], width))  // pen on, teleport
    promise.then(() => console.log("drawing complete"))
    \endqml

    \section1 An action: rotate to an absolute heading

    Rotating the turtle to a heading is a long-running operation with feedback, so
    it is an \e action. \c RotateAbsoluteActionClient.sendGoal() returns a promise
    that resolves with the result, while \c onFeedbackChanged streams progress and
    the \c state property reports the goal's lifecycle:

    \qml
    RotateAbsoluteActionClient {
        id: rotateAction
        node: root.rosNode
        topic: `/${root.baseName}/rotate_absolute`

        onFeedbackChanged: (feedback) =>
            _d.feedbackText = "Remaining: " + feedback.toFixed(3) + " rad"
    }

    // Send a goal and react to the final result
    rotateAction.sendGoal(theta)
        .then(delta => _d.resultText = "Delta: " + delta.toFixed(3) + " rad")
        .catch(error => console.error("Rotation failed:", error))
    \endqml

    The \e{Cancel Rotation} button is bound to the goal state so it is only
    enabled while a goal is in flight:

    \qml
    Button {
        text: "Cancel Rotation"
        enabled: rotateAction.state === RotateAbsoluteActionClient.Accepted
        onClicked: rotateAction.cancelGoal()
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 The UI at a glance

    The window is organized around the currently selected turtle:

    \list
        \li A top row selects a turtle and offers \e Kill and \e{Draw Qt}; a
            second row spawns new turtles by name and position.
        \li \b{Current Turtle Pose} shows the live pose from the \c PoseSubscriber.
        \li \b{Move} provides a directional D-pad (publishing \c Twist velocities),
            plus \e{Move Absolute} and \e{Move Relative} groups that call the
            teleport services.
        \li \b{Absolute heading} sends rotation goals via the action client and
            shows status, feedback, and result.
        \li \b{Pen Settings} sets the pen color (presets or custom RGB), width,
            and on/off state through the \c SetPen service.
    \endlist

    \sa {Simple Publisher}, {Simple Subscriber}, {Qt ROS2 geometry_msgs QML Types}
*/
